## 2. THE THREE GAPS, AND WHAT EACH ONE NOW DOES

### Gap 1 — an unknown caller never reached the CRM

`to_crm_event` emitted `contact_id` only; `client.resolve_contact_id` returns None for a
caller who is in none of the CRM's contacts (which is what "first-time lead" means); and
`deliver_event` then returned `{"ok": false}` and the event was gone. The call was answered,
rung, recorded and filed in OWEN, and the CRM heard nothing.

Now:

  * every body `to_crm_event` builds carries `"from_number": facts.caller_number`, on
    **every phase**, whether or not a `contact_id` was resolved;
  * `contact_id` is **omitted** rather than sent as null when there is none, so a body for a
    resolved contact is byte-for-byte what it was before, plus the one new field;
  * `validate_crm_event` now requires *one of* the two, matching the CRM's own 422;
  * `deliver_event` logs the unresolved caller at INFO and **sends anyway**.

The lookup is still done first. It is the exact match, it is the path the CRM's own tests
pin, and it costs nothing that was not already being spent. `from_number` is the fallback.

A side effect worth naming: a token scoped `events:write` with no `read` scope cannot search
contacts, so the lookup 403s — which used to lose **every event on every call, silently**.
It now delivers through `from_number`. `test_crm_unknown_caller` pins that case by name.

### Gap 2 — inbound SMS was not pushed to the CRM at all

`push.report_call_phase` was called from three places, all in `integrations/crm/handler.py`,
all the bound-DID inbound *call* path. A text to the same number was ingested, relayed to
GoHighLevel and shown in OWEN's own Inbox — and the CRM, which is what the owner works out
of, never saw it, so a reply there had nothing to reply to.

An inbound SMS/MMS on a bound DID now becomes a `type: "SMS"` event on the same
`POST /api/events` endpoint. Nothing new was needed on the CRM side and nothing new was
needed in the queue:

  * `events.MessageEventFacts` + `to_crm_message_event` — the pure map. `INBOUND`, so the
    CRM thread's unread badge is bumped (unlike the machine-written call notes, a
    customer's text IS something a person must answer); never a `call_status` (the CRM 400s
    one on a non-CALL); `provider_ref` = `messages.id`.
  * `push.report_inbound_message` — the **same `crm_report` job**, so retry, linear backoff
    and dead-lettering are the platform's, not a second copy of them.
  * `api.deliver_message_event` (`POST /api/crm-link/message-events`) — the app-side
    adapter, which **re-checks the binding at drain time**. A binding can be disabled
    between enqueue and drain; that is the entire point of a per-number switch, and a job
    already in the queue must not outlive it.
  * `hook.handle_inbound_message` — total, like the call hook: any failure at all returns
    False and the webhook returns its 200 anyway.

The customer's words go across **verbatim**. The only thing ever appended is an MMS note
(`[2 attachments — view in OWEN]`), because the CRM's event row has no media column and on
an MMS the picture of the roof *is* the message.

### Gap 3 — delivery receipts were not pushed

`/webhooks/bulkvs/message-status` advanced OWEN's own `messages` row and returned 200. The
CRM had built and tested `POST /api/events/delivery` and nothing fed it, so its copy of a
message sat on QUEUED forever whether the text arrived, bounced or was blocked.

A receipt for a message **sent through the CRM link on a bound DID** is now relayed. §3 is
the correlation field, which is the part that had to be right.

Two judgements inside it:

  * **The carrier's word is relayed, not OWEN's advanced status.** A receipt is a report of
    what the carrier said. Both sides then apply their own forward-only ladder to it, which
    is what makes a late or out-of-order receipt harmless on either side.
  * **Relayed for every receipt the ladder recognises, not only ones that moved OWEN's own
    row.** `handle_message_send` marks a row `sent` the moment BulkVS accepts it, so a
    carrier `sent` DLR advances nothing here — while being exactly the news the CRM is
    waiting for, because its copy is still QUEUED. Gating on "did OWEN's row move" would
    have left every message stuck at QUEUED in the CRM whenever BulkVS emits no terminal
    DLR, which `services/sms.py` explicitly says it need not do.

