## 4. EVERY FILE TOUCHED OUTSIDE `integrations/crm/`, AND EXACTLY WHY

`git diff main --name-only | grep -v integrations/crm` is five files. **One is production
code; four are tests.**

### `backend/app/webhooks/bulkvs.py` — the only production file

The whole diff is 56 added lines, no line removed or rewritten, and it is three things:

1. **`POST /message` gains two calls.**
   * `crm_hook.is_new_inbound_message(db, sid)` — asked *before* `ingest_message_event`,
     which is an upsert and so cannot report whether it inserted or updated. See §5.2.
   * `crm_hook.handle_inbound_message(...)` — after the `async with SessionLocal()` block
     has closed, so the GoHighLevel relay is already enqueued **and committed** before the
     CRM link runs at all.
2. **`POST /message-status` gains one call**, `crm_hook.handle_delivery_receipt(...)`, after
   OWEN's own row has been advanced and committed.
3. **`_carrier_detail(body)`** — five lines pulling the carrier's failure text out of the
   DLR, so a FAILED message in the CRM says *why*. It lives here rather than in the CRM
   module because it is BulkVS-specific and sits next to the existing, identically shaped
   defensive reads of `RefId` and `Status`.

Why this file and not a different seam: it is the only place an inbound BulkVS message or a
DLR exists. There is no queue, no event bus and no other hook between BulkVS and the
`messages` table. The alternative — a new job type draining the `messages` table on a timer
— would be a second delivery mechanism with a second retry policy and a second thing that
can silently stop working, which is exactly what `push.py` already refuses to build.

Both hook functions are **total**: every failure path returns False, so the webhook's `200`
is reached whatever the CRM is doing. `test_crm_inbound_relay::test_a_broken_crm_push_does_
not_break_message_handling` asserts that with a queue write that raises.

