## 5. THE DATABASE — NO MIGRATION, AND THE TWO PLACES THAT LOOKED LIKE THEY NEEDED ONE

`git diff main --stat -- backend/alembic/` is empty. Your rule — *add new functionality, do
not override the database* — was not bent; it decided both of these.

### 5.1 "Which outbound messages did the CRM send?"

A receipt may only be relayed for a message the CRM itself sent. Anything else is none of
its business, and `POST /api/events/delivery` 404s on a `provider_ref` it has never seen, so
relaying every DID's receipts would be a 404 per operator text, forever.

The obvious answer was a new boolean column on `messages`. I did not add one.

**What I did instead:** a marker written into `messages.raw_payload` (JSONB, already there)
at send time — `{"crm_link": {"link_id": ..., "did": ...}}`, `config.link_marker` /
`config.marker_of`.

Why that is additive and not an override, checked rather than assumed:

  * `grep -rn raw_payload backend/app` is three hits. Two write it
    (`services/messages.ingest_message_event`, `services/ingestion.py`) and **both are
    INBOUND paths**. The third is a comment. **Nothing reads it.**
  * So for an OUTBOUND row `raw_payload` is **always NULL today**. The marker puts a fact
    where there was none. It cannot overwrite a value, because there is no value.
  * `workers/handlers.handle_message_send` rewrites `provider_message_sid` and `status` on a
    successful send and never touches `raw_payload`, so the marker survives to the receipt.
  * It is written only by `api.send_message`, which is the CRM-link SMS route and is
    **dark today** (`CRM_LINK_SMS_ENABLED=false` and the DID's 10DLC campaign is SUBMITTED).
    Until that is lit, this writes nothing at all.

**Why it could not be inferred instead.** "Outbound, on a bound DID, with no
`sent_by_user_id`" was the free alternative and it is wrong: `flows/runtime.py:106` also
sends with `user_id=None`, so a flow's text on a bound DID would be relayed to the CRM as
though the CRM had sent it. `marker_of` is deliberately strict — a non-dict `raw_payload`, a
missing key, or junk under the key all answer "not ours"
(`test_crm_delivery_receipt::test_the_marker_is_what_tells_the_two_kinds_of_message_apart`).

### 5.2 "Has this inbound webhook already been ingested?"

BulkVS re-delivers a POST it did not get a 200 for, `ingest_message_event` is an upsert
keyed on the provider SID, and the CRM's `POST /api/events` has **no dedupe** — so a retry
would put a second copy of the customer's text on their CRM thread. The existing GoHighLevel
relay has exactly this guard in the `relayed_to_ghl` column; I may not add the equivalent.

**What I did instead:** ask before the upsert. `hook.is_new_inbound_message(db, sid)` is one
indexed lookup on the unique `provider_message_sid` column, run *before*
`ingest_message_event`, and it returns False — no push — when the row is already there. It
**makes no query at all** while `CRM_LINK_ENABLED` is false, asserted by counting queries in
`test_crm_inbound_relay::test_the_kill_switch_stops_a_bound_did_too`.

This covers webhook re-delivery. It does **not** cover a `crm_report` job that is retried
after the CRM already inserted the event — that duplicate has been possible for call events
since the module shipped, is documented in `client.py`'s own docstring, and closing it needs
a dedupe key on the CRM's ingest, which is the CRM's side to build. Named here rather than
left to be discovered.

