## 7. THE CRM CONTRACT, AND A DEPLOY-ORDER WARNING

Everything was read out of the CRM's source. The CRM half is on branch
`feature/conversations-live` in `/home/qa/wt/convo` and **is not on ghl-clone's `main`
yet** — `/home/qa/ghl-clone` (main) still declares `contact_id: int` and has no
`/api/events/delivery` at all.

That matters for deploy order, so it is stated plainly:

> **owen-main is built for the amended CRM. Deploying this branch against a CRM that
> predates the amendment is safe but has no effect for the cases it fixes:** an event with
> no `contact_id` is a **422** there (a 4xx — not retryable, logged, job completes, event
> lost exactly as it is lost today), and every delivery receipt is a **404**. Nothing
> regresses; nothing improves either. **Deploy the CRM half first, or at the same time.**

Rather than let that look like a broken contract, the drift checks recognise **both**
versions of the CRM and say which one they found:

  * `test_crm_event_payload::test_the_contract_still_matches_the_crm_source` — against the
    amended source it asserts `contact_id` is optional, `from_number` is declared, and every
    key we send is a field the CRM declares (with and without a `contact_id`). Against a
    pre-amendment source it prints a `[NOTE]` naming the file, asserts `contact_id` is still
    a required int, and asserts the **only** undeclared key we send is `from_number` — the
    one the amendment adds.
  * `test_crm_delivery_receipt::test_the_receipt_contract_still_matches_the_crm_source` —
    same shape, and prints a `[NOTE]` when the checkout has no `DeliveryReceipt` yet.

Point either at the amended tree to see the full check:

    GHL_CLONE_PATH=/home/qa/wt/convo python -m tests.test_crm_event_payload
    GHL_CLONE_PATH=/home/qa/wt/convo python -m tests.test_crm_delivery_receipt

Both pass against both trees. I ran them both ways.

One behaviour of the amended CRM worth knowing before you switch this on: when it creates a
contact for a number it has never seen, it fires its own `on_contact_created` — the new-lead
notification. So a first-time caller or texter on a bound DID will notify the crew through
the CRM. That is the CRM's rule and it is the point of the amendment; it is named here
because it is a thing that will start happening.

I also confirmed the inverse risk is not real: the CRM's `automations.on_inbound_call`
returns early unless `event.type == CALL and direction == INBOUND`, so an SMS event can
never trigger the missed-call auto text-back.

